# Data Quality and Integrity

## Status

This document describes data-quality and temporal-integrity rules currently being implemented in the project.

The following concepts are now part of the model:

```text
MarketIdentity
DataValidationResult
CollectorRun
FreshnessPolicy
FreshnessResult
```

---

## Core Rule

> Data must be valid, semantically equivalent, correctly matched, and temporally coherent before it can participate in anomaly analysis.

---

## Validation Stages

Validation is explicitly divided into:

```text
STRUCTURAL
SEMANTIC
IDENTITY
TEMPORAL
```

### Structural

Checks whether required data exists and can be parsed.

### Semantic

Checks whether values are logically valid.

### Identity

Checks whether the event and market have been resolved correctly.

### Temporal

Checks whether the observations are fresh and comparable in time.

---

## Market Semantics

`MarketIdentity` has been introduced so market equivalence does not depend on a display string.

Potential fields include:

```text
market_type
period
line
rules
specifier
```

Only equivalent market identities may participate in the same comparison.

---

## Timestamp Policy

All internal timestamps are timezone-aware and normalized to UTC.

Important timestamps:

```text
event_start_time
observed_at
source_timestamp
analysis_time
```

`observed_at` is controlled by our system.

`source_timestamp` is optional external metadata and may have source-specific semantics.

---

## Freshness and Temporal Coherence

Current-market analysis must verify:

```text
snapshot age
observation spread
```

A snapshot can be valid historical data while still being invalid for a current comparison.

Freshness thresholds must remain configuration-driven.

---

## Collector Quality

Every ingestion cycle should be represented by `CollectorRun`.

Tracked information includes:

```text
source
started_at
finished_at
status
records_received
records_accepted
records_rejected
collector_version
errors
```

Statuses:

```text
SUCCESS
PARTIAL
FAILED
```

This allows the analysis layer to distinguish complete source coverage from partial ingestion.

---

## DataValidationResult

Validation returns structured results.

Typical contents:

```text
valid
stage
errors
warnings
```

This enables:

- debugging
- metrics
- source-quality monitoring
- rejection reasons
- future review/reprocessing

---

## Data Quality vs Market Anomaly

The engine distinguishes:

```text
DATA_QUALITY_ANOMALY
MARKET_ANOMALY
ARBITRAGE_SIGNAL
SYSTEM_ANOMALY
```

A suspicious value must not automatically become a market signal.

